FILED s513g (Opus, 2026-10-09) from the #2878 guard's design; NOT fixed.  Pre-existing on main b7bf55b9 for a PLAIN module (no overlay involved).

REPRODUCER (module PosK.pm: `package PosK; require Exporter; our @ISA = ('Exporter'); our @EXPORT_OK = qw(KK); sub KK () { 1 } 1;`):

    no strict;
    my $s = KK;          # perl: the string "KK" -- the name is not known yet
    print "$s\n";
    use PosK qw(KK);
    my $t = KK;
    print "$t\n";

perl 5.40.3: `KK` / `1`.  PCL: `1` / `1`.

The PARSE is positional (#2871): above the `use`, `ONE + 1` lowers as `(pl-ONE 1)` -- exactly as with no `use` at all -- and below it as `(p-+ (pl-ONE) 1)`; Pl/t/facts-overlay-01.t row 2 asserts that on the emission.  What differs is the LOWERING of an unknown bareword in a value slot: it is emitted as a call `(pl-KK)`, which finds the sub the `use` installed by run time.  perl decided "string" at parse time.  `use constant k` below its use is handled (proto-position-01.t: '`use constant` below: the bareword is the string above it') -- that is the 'not-yet' answer of `_bareword_callable_here`; an IMPORT below evidently does not reach the same answer at this site.  DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (not taken): ask `_bareword_callable_here` for `KK` at the line-2 site and print its answer -- 'not-yet' means the lowering ignores it, anything else means the import record's site is not consulted.

Probe: s513g worktree scratch/s513g/pr/f9.pl + pr/lib/PosK.pm.

